1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,711
Welcome to Code Quest, Chapter 1.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:06,273
Today you will write and run your first
Python program.

3
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:11,442
You will also learn to use an AI assistant
as a tutor, and to check its work.

4
00:00:12,167 --> 00:00:13,691
Let's start with a puzzle.

5
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:18,385
A learner asked an AI assistant to print
today's date with hyphens between the

6
00:00:18,405 --> 00:00:18,830
numbers.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,409
The AI wrote this single line of code.

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,277
Here is the real output.

9
00:00:34,297 --> 00:00:37,458
There are spaces where the hyphens should
be.

10
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:41,726
The code runs without an error, but it
does not do what was asked.

11
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,900
By the end of this video, you will know
why, and how to fix it.

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,930
Here are your five goals for this chapter.

13
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:49,630
One.

14
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:52,869
Run a Python file and see its output.

15
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:53,577
Two.

16
00:00:53,597 --> 00:00:59,682
Explain how Python runs a program: one
line at a time, from top to bottom.

17
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:00,502
Three.

18
00:01:00,522 --> 00:01:05,052
Use print to show text and numbers, and
control the spacing.

19
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:05,747
Four.

20
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:09,420
Read an error message and find the line
that caused it.

21
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:10,524
And five.

22
00:01:10,544 --> 00:01:14,658
Ask an AI assistant to explain code in a
safe way.

23
00:01:15,233 --> 00:01:17,381
Every app you use is a program.

24
00:01:17,401 --> 00:01:22,135
A program is a list of instructions that a
computer follows exactly.

25
00:01:22,533 --> 00:01:26,295
When a program shows a message, you can
see what it is doing.

26
00:01:26,315 --> 00:01:30,015
Professional developers often use print to
check their code.

27
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,420
AI assistants such as ChatGPT and Claude
can write code.

28
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:36,216
They are often right.

29
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:39,704
But sometimes they are wrong, and still
sound sure.

30
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:45,270
Researchers who built AI tutors for
Harvard's CS50 course saw exactly this.

31
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:47,237
So who checks the code?

32
00:01:58,433 --> 00:01:58,892
You do.

33
00:01:58,912 --> 00:02:03,477
You are the person who runs the code, and
decides if it is correct.

34
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:07,983
A Python program is a text file that ends
in dot p y.

35
00:02:08,003 --> 00:02:10,062
Each line is an instruction.

36
00:02:10,433 --> 00:02:14,245
Python, the program called the
interpreter, reads your file.

37
00:02:14,633 --> 00:02:19,422
It carries out each line in order: top to
bottom, one line at a time.

38
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:24,419
Print is a function: a named action that
Python already knows.

39
00:02:24,439 --> 00:02:27,244
You call it with its name, then brackets.

40
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:30,143
Text must be inside quotation marks.

41
00:02:30,163 --> 00:02:33,165
Text in quotation marks is called a
string.

42
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,146
Think of a program like a recipe.

43
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:38,619
The cook follows each step in order.

44
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,702
But the comparison stops at one point.

45
00:02:41,722 --> 00:02:43,994
A cook can guess what you meant.

46
00:02:44,014 --> 00:02:44,997
Python cannot.

47
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:49,711
If a step has a typing mistake, Python
stops and reports an error.

48
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:51,455
Quick check.

49
00:02:51,475 --> 00:02:53,973
A file has three print lines.

50
00:02:53,993 --> 00:02:57,099
In which order does Python run them?

51
00:02:57,119 --> 00:02:58,836
Choose A, B, C or D.

52
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:11,176
The answer is B.

53
00:03:11,196 --> 00:03:15,611
Python runs your file from top to bottom,
one line at a time.

54
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:18,228
Time for a short breather.

55
00:03:18,248 --> 00:03:19,847
Sit back for a moment.

56
00:03:19,867 --> 00:03:21,982
Here is what you know so far.

57
00:03:22,367 --> 00:03:26,085
Next, you will install Python and write
your first file.

58
00:03:30,667 --> 00:03:33,412
Step 1: install Python.

59
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:39,712
The current version is Python 3.15, and
any version from 3.12 works for this

60
00:03:39,732 --> 00:03:40,258
course.

61
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:45,965
On Windows, use the Python install manager
from python dot org or the Microsoft

62
00:03:45,985 --> 00:03:46,372
Store.

63
00:03:46,767 --> 00:03:51,878
On macOS, download the installer package
from python dot org and open it.

64
00:03:52,267 --> 00:03:55,532
On Linux, Python is usually installed
already.

65
00:03:56,267 --> 00:03:58,636
Then open a terminal and check the
version.

66
00:03:58,967 --> 00:04:02,975
On Windows, type python, space, dash dash
version.

67
00:04:03,367 --> 00:04:07,957
On macOS and Linux, type python3, space,
dash dash version.

68
00:04:07,977 --> 00:04:11,708
You should see a line that starts with
Python 3.

69
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:12,802
Step 2.

70
00:04:12,822 --> 00:04:18,990
Open a code editor, such as Visual Studio
Code or IDLE, and create a file called ex

71
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:19,735
0 1 hello.

72
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:23,826
Line 1 starts with a hash sign, so it is a
comment.

73
00:04:23,846 --> 00:04:25,573
Python ignores comments.

74
00:04:25,593 --> 00:04:27,466
They are notes for people.

75
00:04:27,867 --> 00:04:29,646
Line 2 prints a greeting.

76
00:04:30,033 --> 00:04:31,952
Line 3 prints a second line.

77
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,413
Step 3: run the file.

78
00:04:34,433 --> 00:04:38,953
In the terminal, go to the folder that
holds your file.

79
00:04:39,333 --> 00:04:42,332
On Windows, type python, then the file
name.

80
00:04:42,733 --> 00:04:46,626
On macOS and Linux, type python3, then the
file name.

81
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:48,993
You will see two lines.

82
00:04:49,013 --> 00:04:51,783
Each print produces one line of output.

83
00:04:52,167 --> 00:04:57,228
The quotation marks do not appear, because
they only mark where the text starts and

84
00:04:57,248 --> 00:04:57,534
ends.

85
00:04:58,133 --> 00:04:58,727
Step 4.

86
00:04:58,747 --> 00:05:02,846
This program prints three steps for making
tea.

87
00:05:02,866 --> 00:05:04,862
Step 1, boil the water.

88
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:07,210
Step 2, add the tea leaves.

89
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,960
Step 3, pour the tea into a cup.

90
00:05:10,333 --> 00:05:12,809
Before you run it, predict the output.

91
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,591
The lines appear in the same order as the
code.

92
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:31,038
Try this: swap two lines in your file and
run it again.

93
00:05:31,433 --> 00:05:35,639
The output changes, because Python always
runs from top to bottom.

94
00:05:36,233 --> 00:05:36,799
Step 5.

95
00:05:36,819 --> 00:05:41,740
Print can show several values at once,
separated by commas.

96
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:43,833
It also has two settings.

97
00:05:44,233 --> 00:05:47,036
Sep sets what goes between the values.

98
00:05:47,056 --> 00:05:48,894
Its default is one space.

99
00:05:49,267 --> 00:05:52,287
End sets what goes after the last value.

100
00:05:52,307 --> 00:05:54,264
Its default is a new line.

101
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,211
Look at line 2.

102
00:05:56,231 --> 00:06:00,148
The calculation 3 plus 4 has no quotation
marks.

103
00:06:00,533 --> 00:06:02,789
Line 3 sets sep to a colon.

104
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:07,466
Line 4 sets end to three dots, and line 5
prints the word done.

105
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:20,129
Here is the output.

106
00:06:20,149 --> 00:06:25,905
Line 2 has no quotation marks, so Python
calculates 3 plus 4 and prints 7.

107
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:31,032
Line 3 joins the values with colons
instead of spaces, so they look like a

108
00:06:31,052 --> 00:06:31,354
time.

109
00:06:31,733 --> 00:06:34,999
Line 4 ends with three dots instead of a
new line.

110
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:40,455
So line 5 continues on the same line:
Loading, dot dot dot, done.

111
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:41,945
Quick check.

112
00:06:41,965 --> 00:06:45,768
Which setting changes what goes between
the values in print?

113
00:06:56,967 --> 00:06:58,849
The answer is B, sep.

114
00:06:58,869 --> 00:07:01,024
Sep goes between values.

115
00:07:01,044 --> 00:07:03,742
End goes after the last value.

116
00:07:04,467 --> 00:07:05,531
Another breather.

117
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:09,232
Relax your shoulders, and look at what you
can already do.

118
00:07:09,633 --> 00:07:13,549
Next, you will put it all together in one
complete program.

119
00:07:18,133 --> 00:07:22,452
Here is a complete program that prints a
receipt for the Corner Cafe.

120
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:24,338
Lines 2 to 4 print the header.

121
00:07:24,733 --> 00:07:30,066
Lines 5 and 6 use sep to draw the dotted
lines between each item and its price.

122
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:34,951
Lines 7 and 8 have calculations without
quotation marks.

123
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:37,368
Run it, and you get the receipt.

124
00:07:48,567 --> 00:07:51,091
Items 2, and Total U S D 10.

125
00:07:51,111 --> 00:07:54,818
Python worked out 1 plus 1, and 3 plus 7.

126
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:59,187
Notice one problem: each price is typed
twice.

127
00:07:59,567 --> 00:08:03,831
In Chapter 2, you will store prices in
named values instead.

128
00:08:04,433 --> 00:08:09,655
Use an AI assistant as a tutor that
explains, not as a machine that does your

129
00:08:09,675 --> 00:08:09,996
work.

130
00:08:10,016 --> 00:08:11,834
Follow three safety habits.

131
00:08:12,233 --> 00:08:12,550
One.

132
00:08:12,570 --> 00:08:18,183
Never paste passwords, keys or private
information into an AI tool.

133
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:18,863
Two.

134
00:08:18,883 --> 00:08:25,116
Run every piece of AI code yourself, and
compare the output with what you asked

135
00:08:25,136 --> 00:08:25,433
for.

136
00:08:25,833 --> 00:08:26,238
Three.

137
00:08:26,258 --> 00:08:30,842
You are responsible for the code you use,
even if an AI wrote it.

138
00:08:31,567 --> 00:08:33,323
AI helps at every stage.

139
00:08:33,343 --> 00:08:36,134
Plan the steps in plain English first.

140
00:08:36,533 --> 00:08:40,205
Write the program yourself, then ask for
an explanation.

141
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:43,111
Generate code from an exact output.

142
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:45,523
Debug with the full error message.

143
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:49,688
Test by comparing outputs, space by space.

144
00:08:50,067 --> 00:08:52,671
And improve with at most two suggestions.

145
00:08:53,067 --> 00:08:53,952
Try this one.

146
00:09:05,133 --> 00:09:05,488
Debug.

147
00:09:05,508 --> 00:09:10,490
Paste the code and the full error message,
and ask for the cause before the fix.

148
00:09:11,233 --> 00:09:12,754
Here is the Write card.

149
00:09:12,774 --> 00:09:16,103
Paste your code, and ask the AI not to
rewrite it.

150
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:21,141
Ask it to explain each line in one simple
sentence, and to say whether it prints

151
00:09:21,161 --> 00:09:22,773
exactly the output you want.

152
00:09:23,167 --> 00:09:24,688
Then run your program.

153
00:09:24,708 --> 00:09:29,244
If the AI said yes but the output differs,
trust the real output.

154
00:09:29,967 --> 00:09:32,173
And here is the Generate card.

155
00:09:32,193 --> 00:09:34,697
Ask for code that uses only print.

156
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:39,271
Paste the exact output you want, with the
same spaces and symbols.

157
00:09:39,667 --> 00:09:43,433
Then run it and compare every character,
including spaces.

158
00:09:43,453 --> 00:09:45,848
This is how the date flaw was caught.

159
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:48,922
Now, back to the opening puzzle.

160
00:09:48,942 --> 00:09:53,095
The request asked for hyphens, but the
output has spaces.

161
00:10:04,267 --> 00:10:07,184
The AI forgot that sep is a space by
default.

162
00:10:07,567 --> 00:10:11,355
The fix sets sep to a hyphen, and now the
date prints correctly.

163
00:10:11,733 --> 00:10:16,473
Always check AI output against what you
asked for, character by character.

164
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:18,098
Take a breath.

165
00:10:18,118 --> 00:10:20,328
You have caught a real AI mistake.

166
00:10:20,348 --> 00:10:23,476
That is the most important skill in this
course.

167
00:10:23,867 --> 00:10:27,492
Next, the four mistakes that beginners
make most often.

168
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:32,820
Mistake 1.

169
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:35,604
This line should print Good morning.

170
00:10:35,624 --> 00:10:37,228
Something is missing.

171
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:50,986
The closing quotation mark is missing.

172
00:10:51,006 --> 00:10:55,650
Python cannot see where the text ends, so
it reports a syntax error.

173
00:10:56,033 --> 00:11:00,611
A syntax error means Python could not read
the file, so no line runs.

174
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,045
Add the closing quotation mark before the
bracket.

175
00:11:04,767 --> 00:11:05,853
Mistake 2.

176
00:11:05,873 --> 00:11:07,512
Line 2 is fine.

177
00:11:07,532 --> 00:11:10,056
Look closely at line 3.

178
00:11:21,233 --> 00:11:23,214
Print starts with a capital P.

179
00:11:23,234 --> 00:11:27,682
Python treats capital P Print and small p
print as different names.

180
00:11:28,067 --> 00:11:30,251
Notice that Start was printed first.

181
00:11:30,271 --> 00:11:34,965
This error happened while the program was
running, so the lines before it had

182
00:11:34,985 --> 00:11:35,699
already run.

183
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:40,828
The report names line 3, and the last line
even suggests the fix.

184
00:11:41,567 --> 00:11:44,061
Mistake 3 is curly quotation marks.

185
00:11:44,081 --> 00:11:50,024
Word processors and some chat apps change
straight quotation marks into curly ones.

186
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:52,656
Python only accepts straight ones.

187
00:11:53,033 --> 00:11:56,078
Type the quotation marks again in your
code editor.

188
00:11:56,467 --> 00:12:00,522
And copy code from a code block, never
from a formatted document.

189
00:12:01,267 --> 00:12:02,110
Mistake 4.

190
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:04,871
One more line that will not run.

191
00:12:16,067 --> 00:12:18,048
The closing bracket is missing.

192
00:12:18,068 --> 00:12:21,017
Every opening bracket needs a closing
bracket.

193
00:12:21,767 --> 00:12:23,953
Here are all four, side by side.

194
00:12:23,973 --> 00:12:27,124
Each one is the last line of its error
report.

195
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:31,696
Unterminated string literal: a quotation
mark is missing.

196
00:12:32,067 --> 00:12:36,588
Name is not defined: check the spelling,
such as a capital P.

197
00:12:36,967 --> 00:12:40,756
Invalid character: retype the quotation
marks.

198
00:12:41,133 --> 00:12:44,157
Was never closed: add the closing bracket.

199
00:12:44,533 --> 00:12:47,681
The habit to build: read the last line
first.

200
00:12:47,701 --> 00:12:49,582
It names the kind of error.

201
00:12:49,602 --> 00:12:51,624
Then look at the line number.

202
00:12:52,367 --> 00:12:53,220
Quick check.

203
00:12:53,240 --> 00:12:55,766
An error message has several lines.

204
00:12:55,786 --> 00:12:57,876
Which line do you read first?

205
00:13:09,067 --> 00:13:11,313
The answer is B, the last line.

206
00:13:11,333 --> 00:13:13,286
It names the kind of error.

207
00:13:14,033 --> 00:13:15,230
One more breather.

208
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:19,216
Errors are normal, and every developer
sees them every day.

209
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:22,819
Next, two questions to think about on your
own.

210
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:30,171
Two questions to think beyond this
chapter.

211
00:13:30,191 --> 00:13:30,560
First.

212
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:35,752
You want to print a shopping list with
each item on its own line, using only one

213
00:13:35,772 --> 00:13:36,142
print.

214
00:13:36,162 --> 00:13:37,764
Which setting could help?

215
00:13:48,967 --> 00:13:51,756
Test your idea in your editor after the
video.

216
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:52,955
Second.

217
00:13:52,975 --> 00:13:56,006
An AI gives you code that runs with no
error.

218
00:13:56,026 --> 00:13:59,600
What two things should you check before
you trust it?

219
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:12,052
Keep your answer.

220
00:14:12,072 --> 00:14:14,520
You will use it in every chapter.

221
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:16,067
Let's recap.

222
00:14:16,467 --> 00:14:21,195
A program is a list of instructions, and
Python runs them from top to bottom.

223
00:14:21,567 --> 00:14:23,056
Print shows values.

224
00:14:23,076 --> 00:14:28,456
Text goes in quotation marks, and
calculations are worked out first.

225
00:14:28,833 --> 00:14:34,061
Sep controls what goes between values, and
end controls what goes after them.

226
00:14:34,433 --> 00:14:38,930
An error names the kind of error and the
line, so read the last line first.

227
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:40,600
And AI is a tutor.

228
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:44,854
Ask it to explain, then run and check
everything yourself.

229
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:47,209
One last quick check.

230
00:14:47,229 --> 00:14:50,699
An AI gives you code that runs with no
error.

231
00:14:50,719 --> 00:14:52,561
What should you do next?

232
00:15:03,733 --> 00:15:04,870
The answer is B.

233
00:15:04,890 --> 00:15:08,847
Run it, and compare the output with what
you asked for.

234
00:15:09,567 --> 00:15:13,093
You earned 100 XP and the First Run badge.

235
00:15:13,113 --> 00:15:13,937
Well done.

236
00:15:16,033 --> 00:15:17,016
Now practise.

237
00:15:17,036 --> 00:15:19,637
The practice file has eight tasks.

238
00:15:20,033 --> 00:15:23,009
The three warm-up tasks are worth 10 XP
each.

239
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:26,515
The three apply tasks are worth 20 XP
each.

240
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:29,439
The two stretch tasks are worth 40 each.

241
00:15:29,459 --> 00:15:34,173
In the last one, you check an AI's work
and fix every difference yourself.

242
00:15:34,567 --> 00:15:37,449
Try each task before you open the
solution.

243
00:15:38,033 --> 00:15:43,081
In Chapter 2, you will give your data
names with variables, so a program can

244
00:15:43,101 --> 00:15:45,815
store a value once and use it many times.

245
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:47,999
Do the warm-up tasks now.

246
00:15:48,019 --> 00:15:50,036
They take about ten minutes.

247
00:15:50,433 --> 00:15:51,876
See you in Chapter 2.
